+/*
+ * ARGON: the encounter tables in a hundred and eighteen kilobytes instead of
+ * four hundred and six.
+ *
+ * Counted from worldmap.txt itself rather than estimated, 7 September 2026:
+ * 1695 encounter table entries, 2891 sub-entries between them, 2334 condition
+ * entries, 370 encounter base type entries and 563 items.  The engine declares
+ * every one of those at its worst case inside the entry that owns it -
+ * subEntries[6], entries[3], items[10] - so the arena holds room for 10170
+ * sub-entries to store 2891, and for 3700 items to store 563.
+ *
+ *     table entries  1695 x 180 = 297 KB
+ *     base entries    370 x 300 = 108 KB
+ *
+ * Patch 0020 already took the same waste out one level up, where the tables
+ * were declared entries[41] and use 22.3.  This is that idea applied to the
+ * three arrays inside an entry: they move into pools shared by every entry,
+ * and an entry says where its own run starts.
+ *
+ * An *index* rather than a pointer, because the pools grow by realloc while
+ * the file is parsed and a pointer would not survive the move.  The index is
+ * uint16_t and the worst case worldmap.txt could ask for is 76 tables x 40
+ * entries x 6 sub-entries = 18240, so the type has three times the room it
+ * needs; wm_pool_grow says so out loud if that ever stops being true.
+ *
+ * The pooled structures are narrowed at the same time, because they are read
+ * through four accessors and nothing takes the address of a field: a count of
+ * critters, a condition operator and an item quantity do not need 32 bits.
+ * The fields of the entries themselves are left as int - they are passed to
+ * strParseIntWithKey by address, and a narrower field there is a compile error
+ * for every one of a dozen call sites, for 17 KB.
+ *
+ *     table entries  1695 x  32 =  53 KB
+ *     base entries    370 x  48 =  17 KB
+ *     sub-entries    2891 x   8 =  23 KB
+ *     conditions     2334 x   8 =  18 KB
+ *     items           563 x  12 =   7 KB
+ *
+ * 288 KB, on every map, from startup to exit.  The temple peaked at 5977 KB of
+ * 6000 and a save load at 5940, and this is what those had left over.
+ */
 typedef struct EncounterConditionEntry {
-    int type;
-    int conditionalOperator;
-    int param;
-    int value;
+    int8_t type;
+    int8_t conditionalOperator;
+    int16_t param;
+    int32_t value;
 } EncounterConditionEntry;
 
+/*
+ * A run in wmCondPool, not three entries of its own.  `first` means nothing
+ * while entriesLength is zero, and 39% of table entries have no condition.
+ *
+ * logicalOperators is [3] where the engine had [2], and that is a fix rather
+ * than a rounding up.  wmParseConditional increments entriesLength to 3 and
+ * *then* looks for another "and" in the rest of the line, writing
+ * logicalOperators[entriesLength - 1] - so index 2, past the end - and
+ * wmEvalConditional reads the same index back.  The engine's own comment says
+ * "FIXME: Can overflow with all 3 conditions specified".  With the old layout
+ * that wrote into subEntiesLength, which followed the condition inside the
+ * entry; with this one the condition is the entry's last member and it would
+ * write into the *next* entry.  Two bytes each is the cheapest possible answer
+ * to that, and it makes the read give back what the write put there.
